    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter tonight\u2019s slate with a potent offensive profile, anchored by **Yordan Alvarez** (75.47) and **Lars Nootbaar** (67.77). **Yordan Alvarez** remains the premier play against Martin; despite a marginal park factor penalty, his elite xwOBA of 0.452 and favorable platoon split make him our highest-floor producer. **Lars Nootbaar** benefits significantly from the day's best dynamic platoon projection (+11%) and the primary leadoff spot against Painter, positioning him for a massive run-scoring night. Meanwhile, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (64.03) presents a fascinating tactical case; while he faces a formidable arm in Misiorowski (2.19 xERA), his massive platoon edge (+14%) and the opportunity to ignite the offense from the leadoff position make him a high-reward start in our hunt for stolen bases.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today involved the marginal range where **Caleb Durbin** (55.01) and **Gary Sanchez** (55.14) barely clung to their spots. **Caleb Durbin** was kept in the lineup specifically for his utility in our scarcity-driven SB category; even against Miller, his speed profile and the benefit of a slow opposing delivery time (+2%) narrowly outweighed the potential upside of sitting him. On the bench, **Christian Yelich** and **Christian Encarnacion-Strand** were excluded primarily due to lineup absences and unfavorable platoon dynamics, with **Christian Yelich**\u2019s negative dynamic platoon against a southpaw creating a significant hurdle for his insertion into the daily rotation.\n\nManagers should monitor the risk profile of **Junior Caminero** (51.25). While he occupies a high-order spot, he faces an elite skill set from Hagenman that suppressed his score significantly (-19% SP Skill). Furthermore, **Carson Benge** (42.61) represents our most volatile start; his lack of a strong platoon advantage combined with a tough pitching matchup puts him at the bottom of our efficiency threshold. We are relying on 